A closure lets an inner function 'remember' variables from the scope it was defined in, even after that outer function has returned. Example: `function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return {
    increment: () => ++count,
    getValue: () => count
  };
}
const counter = makeCounter();
counter.increment();
counter.getValue(); // 1`. The variable `count` isn't accessible from outside directly — it's only reachable through the returned functions, which both close over the same `count` binding, giving effectively private state without needing a class.

Synchronous code runs to completion in order, blocking the thread; asynchronous code lets slow operations happen in the background without freezing everything else. Example: 'async function getUser(id) {
  try {
    const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
    return await res.json();
  } catch (err) {
    console.error("Failed to fetch user:", err);
    throw err;
  }
}'. The try/catch here reads naturally, catching both network failures and the explicitly thrown error for a bad HTTP status, unlike a raw Promise chain needing .catch().

Debouncing waits for a pause in activity before running — useful for a search box that shouldn't fire an API call on every keystroke, only once typing stops. Throttling instead guarantees a function runs at most once per interval no matter how often it's triggered — useful for a scroll handler that shouldn't run on every single scroll event. Debounce implementation: 'function debounce(fn, delay) {
  let timer;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), delay);
  };
}'. Each call resets the timer, so fn only actually runs once calls stop coming in for `delay` milliseconds.

Memoization caches the output of a pure function for a given set of inputs, so a repeated call with the same arguments returns the cached result instantly instead of recomputing. Implementation: 'function memoize(fn) {
  const cache = new Map();
  return (...args) => {
    const key = JSON.stringify(args);
    if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
    const result = fn(...args);
    cache.set(key, result);
    return result;
  };
}'. This only works correctly for pure functions — no reliance on external mutable state and no side effects — and for high-cardinality inputs, an unbounded cache can grow indefinitely, so a real implementation might need an eviction policy like LRU.
